    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s north coast, c. 100–800 CE, whose lords ruled irrigated desert valleys from huge adobe platforms such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-del-sol">Huaca del Sol</a>.</li><li>Its potters made lifelike portrait vessels and painted scenes of combat and sacrifice; its smiths plated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</li><li>Royal tombs at Sipán and El Brujo reveal warrior-priests and powerful women; climate crises and unrest ended it around 800.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>One Moche man, nicknamed ‘Cut Lip’ by the archaeologist Christopher Donnan for the scar on his lip, survives in some forty portrait vessels showing him from boyhood to middle age.</p></aside>
    </section>

