    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche.jpg" alt="Huacas de Moche" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huaca_Sol_lou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Moche, or Mochica, culture took shape around 100 CE in the valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s north coast, a desert crossed by short rivers running down from the Andes. Its heartland lay in the Moche and Chicama valleys near modern Trujillo, and its art and temples eventually reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piura">Piura</a> valley in the north to the Nepeña valley in the south. The Moche left no writing, and their name for themselves is unknown: ‘Moche’ comes from the valley and village beside their greatest monuments, while the alternative name Mochica is borrowed from a language recorded on the north coast in colonial times. They built on older coastal traditions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupisnique">Cupisnique</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salinar">Salinar</a>, as local elites gathered followers around new temple platforms and a shared art of gods, warriors and rituals. Rafael Larco Hoyle divided the southern pottery into five phases, still used for dating, while the northern valleys followed a sequence of their own. Most scholars no longer see the Moche as one centralised state. In the south a polity centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a>, at the foot of Cerro Blanco, probably came to rule several valleys, whereas in the Jequetepeque and Lambayeque valleys to the north separate lordships shared Moche religion and art.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moche prosperity rested on irrigation. Canals carried river water across the valley floors and turned desert into fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans, squash and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, while fishermen in reed boats, often shown on Moche pottery, worked the rich coastal waters. Society was sharply ranked. Lords and priests were buried with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> regalia, while ordinary people were laid in simple graves near their homes. Between the two great platforms at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a> lay a planned town of streets, plazas and walled compounds that housed at least 5,000 people, among them potters whose workshops turned out moulded vessels for ritual use. Moche ceramics are the culture’s best-known product: stirrup-spout bottles, fineline paintings of myths, battles and ceremonies, and portrait heads so individual that Christopher Donnan, studying more than 900 of them, could follow some men from boyhood to middle age. Metalworkers coated copper objects with gold and silver by electrochemical replacement plating and depletion gilding. Spondylus shell, turquoise and lapis lazuli reached the coast from distant regions through long-distance exchange. Religion was bound up with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, and fanged gods appear throughout the art, among them the being known today as the Decapitator, who often holds a knife and a severed head.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-cao"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lady-of-cao.jpg" alt="Lady of Cao" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-cao">Lady of Cao</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstitution_de_la_Dame_de_Cao_(Mus%C3%A9e_du_quai_Branly_-_Jacques_Chirac,_Paris).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean-Pierre Dalbéra from Paris, France, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Warfare and its aftermath fill Moche art. Fineline paintings show warriors in helmets and tunics fighting with conical-headed war clubs, small round or square shields, spear-throwers and darts, usually in single combat; the victor strips his opponent, puts a rope around his neck and leads him away. The captives then appear in the scene known as the Sacrifice Ceremony or Presentation Theme, in which their blood is collected in goblets and offered to a richly dressed figure, the Warrior Priest. Archaeology has shown that such rites really took place. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-de-la-luna">Huaca de la Luna</a>, Steve Bourget excavated the remains of some seventy adolescent and young men who had been killed and dismembered beside a rocky outcrop, some of them left lying in mud laid down by heavy rains. Weapons accompanied elite burials, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-cao">Lady of Cao</a> was buried with war clubs and spear-throwers. Whether Moche combat was chiefly a ritual contest between elite warriors or real warfare between rival polities is still debated. The southern polity probably extended its rule by force over the Virú, Santa and Nepeña valleys in its middle phases, and defensive walls and fortified settlements became common again in the troubled late period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan.jpg" alt="Lord of Sipán" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan">Lord of Sipán</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Se%C3%B1or_de_S%C3%ADpan_-_Reconstrucci%C3%B3n_Facial_Forense.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cicero Moraes, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Without writing, Moche government has to be read from monuments, tombs and images. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-del-sol">Huaca del Sol</a>, built of well over 100 million adobe bricks and still about 40 metres high, probably served as a palace, administrative platform and burial place, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-de-la-luna">Huaca de la Luna</a>, rebuilt in successive stages and painted with murals, was the main temple. More than a hundred different makers’ marks on the bricks of the Huaca del Sol suggest that many communities owed labour and delivered their quotas of bricks, a system archaeologists have compared with the rotating labour service known under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> as <em>mit’a</em>. Rulers combined military, religious and political roles. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan">Lord of Sipán</a>, buried around 250 CE in the Lambayeque valley with ornaments of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, wore regalia matching that of the Warrior Priest of the Sacrifice Ceremony, and the tombs of women excavated at San José de Moro from 1991 by Christopher Donnan and Luis Jaime Castillo matched the Priestess of the same scene. This suggests that the figures in the art were offices held in turn by real people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-of-cao">Lady of Cao</a>, who died around 450 CE, shows that a woman could hold the highest rank, perhaps ruling a valley.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chan-chan.jpg" alt="Chan Chan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chan_chan_view1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Håkan Svensson, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Moche produced the most naturalistic art of the ancient Andes, and their pottery, murals and metalwork are among the fullest sources for life, belief and ritual in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>. Their portrait vessels, made in quantity by no other ancient American people, preserve the faces of individual men, and fineline scenes record hunting, weaving, farming, ritual and myth. Their skills passed to the peoples who followed on the north coast: the Sicán of the Lambayeque valley built on Moche metalworking, and the Chimú raised their capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chan-chan">Chan Chan</a>, in the same Moche valley, continuing traditions of adobe platforms, irrigation and moulded pottery. The Moche were also central to the modern rediscovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s past. Max Uhle excavated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a> in 1899–1900, and Rafael Larco Hoyle, whose collection forms the Museo Larco in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, defined the culture and its phases. The intact tomb of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-sipan">Lord of Sipán</a>, excavated by Walter Alva in 1987 after looters had found gold at Huaca Rajada, was one of the great archaeological discoveries of the late twentieth century and focused attention on the damage done by looting. The Royal Tombs of Sipán Museum in Lambayeque opened in 2002, and excavations at the Huacas de Moche have continued since 1991.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pampa-grande"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pampa-grande.jpg" alt="Pampa Grande" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pampa-grande">Pampa Grande</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pir%C3%A1mide_en_complejo_arqueol%C3%B3gico_moche_de_Pampa_Grande,_Chongoyape,_Lambayeque,_Per%C3%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MI PERÚ, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Moche world was hit hard in the sixth century. Ice cores from the Quelccaya ice cap in the southern Peruvian Andes record a severe drought between about 562 and 594, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a> rains and floods damaged canals and fields on the coast in the same period. Political change followed. In the late phase, Moche V, new centres grew at Galindo, inland in the Moche valley, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampa-grande">Pampa Grande</a> in the Lambayeque valley, a town of more than 400 hectares dominated by the platform of Huaca Fortaleza. Galindo was divided by high walls, and fortified settlements appeared in the Jequetepeque valley, signs of tension within Moche society. Garth Bawden has argued that rulers without a formal administration or a standing army could not maintain their legitimacy against the communal traditions of Andean society. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huacas-de-moche">Huacas de Moche</a> lost their importance, and soon after 700 the houses and stored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and beans of Pampa Grande were burned, probably in a revolt, and the town was abandoned. Galindo was given up around 800, and only at San José de Moro did Moche burial traditions continue a little longer, until about 850. On the north coast the Sicán culture then rose in the Lambayeque valley, while the Chimú emerged in the Moche valley itself.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Moche were the great civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>’s north coast in the first eight centuries CE, irrigating desert valleys, raising the largest adobe platform in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and making portrait vessels and gilded metalwork of striking realism, while royal tombs such as Sipán reveal the warrior-priest rulers whose sacrificial rites their art depicts.</em></p>

